{
  "_comment": "The turnaround of each kind of aeroplane, read by wilcoatc/atc/handling.py. 'types' says which profile an ICAO type uses (anything else: 'airliner' or 'light'; a business jet is 'business', fuel only, because it boards by its own airstair and has no hold worked by a loader -- no stairs, loader, caterer or bags go to it, nor to a 'light' aircraft, which gets a fuel truck at most). A round is a list of steps: 'service' is the vehicle, 'after' the moment it waits for ('start': the round begins -- the aeroplane stopped, was found, or began boarding; 'deboarded': the last passenger is off, which is the stairs at the door plus deboard_s, or the bus's last walker if one came), 'delay_s' how long after that it sets off, 'serve_s' how long it stays once parked (null: until the departure closes up). Every time is jittered by 20% and divided by traffic.debug.time_scale. 'fuel_with_passengers': whether the fuel truck may work while passengers board; if not, boarding sends it away. 'footprints': the ground each parked vehicle covers, in its own frame (metres ahead of and behind its reference, half its width), which is what another vehicle must keep clear of. 'margin_m' is added all round. 'baggage': how many bags a flight has (wilcoatc/atc/baggage.py bags_for): seats (per ICAO type, else per profile) x a load factor drawn between the two given x bags a passenger -- 'low_cost' for the airlines listed there, 'long_haul' for a widebody, 'legacy' otherwise -- with 'spread' either way, 'forward_share' of them in the forward hold. GSX's handler moves them a cart of eleven at a time.",
